# Observability bench spec — {window_id}

> **Generated** by the repo bench on {written_at}.
> Canonical source: `data/repo_bench/spec/{window_id}__{view_id}.json`.
> This file is regenerated on every benchmark run; **do not hand-edit**.

This is a cross-module spec. The repo bench module defines the
contract; the observability bench module produces the `RawTelemetry`
bundle. Treat each section as a hard requirement.

## 1. Inputs (what to bench)

**vLLM SHA**: `{snapshot_sha}` (full hash)

- Merge of [PR #{snapshot_pr_number}](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/{snapshot_pr_number})
- Merged at: {snapshot_merged_at}
- GitHub commit: <https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/commit/{snapshot_sha}>

**Why this SHA** (do not silently substitute — see §3):

{rationale}

**Config notes**: {config_notes}

## 2. Output (interface contract)

**Where**: a new directory under shared storage, named:

```
{output_naming_template}
```

Where:
- `<timestamp>` = UTC at job start, format `YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ`.
- `<config>` = a tag identifying the workload/config used for this run.
- `<short_sha>` = first 7 chars of `{snapshot_sha}` → `{snapshot_sha_short}`.

**What goes in it**: a `RawTelemetry` bundle — traces, metrics, logs
captured end-to-end against this SHA on the chosen workload. If
something is missing because the SHA broke a wrap target, **flag it**
in the run report rather than silently omitting it.

## 3. Failure protocol

- If the SHA won't build / install / run, surface the failure to the
  repo-bench side with concrete evidence: build log, missing
  class, dependency conflict, etc.
- **Do NOT silently substitute a different SHA.** Substitution breaks
  the experimental contract — the benchmark is bound to *this* SHA,
  not "a recent SHA in the same range."
- We can debug shim/build issues together; don't work around them
  alone.

## 4. Sync note

The repo-bench module also clones vLLM at this SHA on its own
side (for static analysis input to the signal pipeline). The SHA pin
is the synchronization point between modules — if we have to change
it, both sides re-pin.

## 5. The contract

This spec is what repo-bench needs from the observability
bench module to produce a scored result. After the bench produces a
`RawTelemetry` bundle:

1. The repo-bench side runs the signal pipeline against the
   `RawTelemetry` + target-repo source at `{snapshot_sha}` to produce
   `findings.json`.
2. We run `repo-bench match --findings <findings.json> ...`
   to grade against the filtered view ({n_view} PRs all merged after
   {snapshot_merged_at}).
3. We write up the result.

That last step doesn't depend on the bench module. Its contract ends
at the `RawTelemetry` bundle.
